The age at marriage is an important proximate determinant of fertility. Women who marry at an early age 
often have more children than those marrying later.  
 
The higher proportion of young married women compared with men of the same age indicates that 
women generally marry at y ounger ages than men (Table 22 and Fig.37 ). The average age at marriage 
(Singulate mean age at marriage, SMAM) was 24.4 and 22.6  years for males and females, respectively, 
and was calculated based on the proportion of those never married/single by age.  
 
While only 4% of males were married at ages 15-19, it was about 13% of females (Fig.38). At age 20-24 
almost half of all women were already married compared with 37 % of males. Compared to earlier 
censuses, the percentage of males and females married at young ages has declined, while the average age 
53 
 
at marriage decreased especially for females from 1992 to 2002, but remained  fairly constant since then 
(Table 22 and Figs.37).  
 
