4.8.3 Labour force participation rate, employment–population ratio, and unemployment 
rate 
 
The labour force participation rate (LFPR)  is the number of people in the labour force by a given age 
and sex and/or place of rural –urban re sidence, divided by the corresponding total population with the 
same characteristics, multiplied by 100. 
 
The employment–population ratio (EPR)  is the number of people employed in cash work by a given 
age and sex and/or place of rural –urban residence, divided by the corresponding total population with the 
same characteristics, multiplied by 100. 
 
The unemployment rate is the number of people unemployed by a given age and sex and/or place of 
rural–urban residence, divided by the population in the labor force  with the same characteristics, 
multiplied by 100. 
 
The LFPR is calculated at 64% in Nauru in 2011. At the  same time the EPR was only 46.7 %, and the 
unemployment rate for Nauru was 23% (Table 33). 
 
The LFPRs were higher for males (78.9%) than for females ( 49.3%), so was the employment-
population ratio with 58.2% and 35.2% for males and females respectively. 
 
The unemployment rate was higher for females (25.5%) than males (21.4%). 
 
